{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call-1f105527-5fd9-4680-8740-3e76e0f3eb9d-2","type":"tool_result","content":"1	//! Change the hub by talking (ash-day step 15): a Mono hands Ash's words to a Claude Code worker, which edits a
2	//! preview copy of the hub's own repository (a git worktree under `<app data>/hub-changes/<id>`, one commit per
3	//! separate change). Ash sees Now | Preview in the running hub, then Apply or Discard; each applied part can be
4	//! undone on its own.
5	//!
6	//! Two lanes, one rule (Ash 22 Oct: "it can improve itself n its ui yes but the core code is diff"): the app's own
7	//! look is changed here, in the app Ash runs; the engine is not. The core app stays protected here, not in a prompt: a
8	//! change that touches anything outside `src/` and `public/`, the engine (Rust, the provider and platform layer, the
9	//! chat engine, the hub's own store, its agent tools), settings, keys, the Monos list, start-up or this flow itself,
10	//! is refused with the area named in plain words and goes to the MonoCode Hub lead instead (a worktree, checks, a dev
11	//! copy, then Ship).
12	//!
13	//! Two ways a change reaches the app, because a dev copy and an installed bundle are different things ([`Delivery`]):
14	//! - A dev copy runs from its worktree under vite. Preview puts the change into that worktree uncommitted
15	//!   (`git apply`) and Now takes it out, so it shows at once; Apply cherry-picks it there.
16	//! - The installed app runs from a bundle whose frontend is compiled in, so nothing it does can hot-reload. Preview
17	//!   starts vite in the change's worktree and opens `hub-dev.html` in the browser: the changed screens with example
18	//!   data, in a page that can't strand the real window. Apply puts the commits on the checkout's own branch (the
19	//!   "main repo") and hands that commit to the existing ship path ([`crate::ship`], `npm run hub:ship`): checks, a
20	//!   build, a staged trial that commits or rolls itself back. Nothing reaches the installed app any other way. The
21	//!   browser is chosen over shipping a trial as the preview because it costs no build, touches no data Ash keeps, and
22	//!   a broken change can only break a page that closes with the browser.
23	//!
24	//! An in-place preview that breaks the window can't strand Ash: the window pings every few seconds while it previews,
25	//! and a watchdog takes the preview out after [`PING_TIMEOUT`] without one, also after a crash (`live.json`).
